1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,703
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,723 --> 00:00:08,444
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,844 --> 00:00:13,489
This is Lecture 6, the feed mechanism.

4
00:00:13,889 --> 00:00:19,559
On every stitch, the feed dog under the throat plate rises to bite the cloth,

5
00:00:19,579 --> 00:00:23,475
pushes it forward a little, then drops and goes back.

6
00:00:23,495 --> 00:00:27,909
Push too early or too late, or let the two plies move apart,

7
00:00:27,929 --> 00:00:33,376
and you get uneven stitches, a growing upper ply, or even a broken needle.

8
00:00:34,896 --> 00:00:38,436
Here's the problem.

9
00:00:38,836 --> 00:00:44,400
A jeans factory is sewing waistbands: four plies of denim,

10
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:49,599
about 4 millimetres thick, with a 3 millimetre stitch.

11
00:00:49,999 --> 00:00:56,246
The machine is set to the manual's standard feed timing and has always been

12
00:00:56,266 --> 00:00:58,670
fine on 1.5 millimetre cloth.

13
00:00:59,070 --> 00:01:04,050
But on the waistband the needle is dragged sideways and breaks from time to time.

14
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:07,240
When does the point enter such thick cloth?

15
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:12,636
How far is the cloth pushed after the needle is in?

16
00:01:13,036 --> 00:01:17,136
How should the feed timing change, and by how much?

17
00:01:17,536 --> 00:01:19,836
We'll work it out at the end.

18
00:01:21,356 --> 00:01:23,056
What the feed has to do.

19
00:01:24,576 --> 00:01:34,316
What the feed must do was patented by Wilson in 1854: the four-motion feed.

20
00:01:34,716 --> 00:01:40,157
The dog rises to grip the cloth, advances carrying it forward,

21
00:01:40,177 --> 00:01:44,914
drops below the throat plate and returns to its start.

22
00:01:45,314 --> 00:01:51,362
Watch the side view from Lab 6-1; the green part is the feed dog.

23
00:01:51,762 --> 00:01:57,470
Rise and drop decide when the cloth is gripped and released;

24
00:01:57,490 --> 00:02:00,715
advance and return decide how far.

25
00:02:01,115 --> 00:02:06,317
The key point: only the advance above the plate moves the cloth;

26
00:02:06,337 --> 00:02:09,499
the return underneath doesn't touch it.

27
00:02:11,019 --> 00:02:15,510
The first constraint: while the needle is in the cloth,

28
00:02:15,530 --> 00:02:17,479
the cloth must not move.

29
00:02:17,879 --> 00:02:22,053
This is the timing chart; across is the shaft angle,

30
00:02:22,073 --> 00:02:24,876
the steep line is the needle point,

31
00:02:24,896 --> 00:02:29,150
and the grey band is when the needle is in the cloth.

32
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:33,381
From Lecture 3's slider-crank formula,

33
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:41,488
with 1.5 millimetre cloth the point enters at 101.7 degrees and leaves at 258.3,

34
00:02:41,508 --> 00:02:46,251
leaving about two hundred degrees for the feed.

35
00:02:46,651 --> 00:02:53,066
Push the cloth with the needle in, and you get crooked, uneven stitches,

36
00:02:53,086 --> 00:02:56,551
or the needle hits the hook and breaks.

37
00:02:58,071 --> 00:03:01,119
Structurally there are two drive trains.

38
00:03:01,519 --> 00:03:03,497
Green is the horizontal one:

39
00:03:03,517 --> 00:03:07,635
the feed eccentric on the upper shaft drives the feed rod,

40
00:03:07,655 --> 00:03:12,630
through the stitch regulator, a tilting guide, to the feed rock shaft,

41
00:03:12,650 --> 00:03:15,412
which moves the dog bar back and forth.

42
00:03:15,812 --> 00:03:18,142
Amber is the vertical one:

43
00:03:18,162 --> 00:03:24,018
the lift eccentric on the lower shaft drives the lift rock shaft,

44
00:03:24,038 --> 00:03:27,271
which raises and lowers the dog bar.

45
00:03:27,671 --> 00:03:30,869
Add the two and you have the dog's motion.

46
00:03:31,269 --> 00:03:36,628
The benefit: stitch length changes only the horizontal amplitude,

47
00:03:36,648 --> 00:03:39,359
not when the dog rises and drops;

48
00:03:39,379 --> 00:03:44,490
dog height changes only the vertical position, not the stitch.

49
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:48,909
The dog's path and the feed per stitch.

50
00:03:50,429 --> 00:03:51,452
Now a model.

51
00:03:51,852 --> 00:03:56,592
A link driven by an eccentric moves almost harmonically.

52
00:03:56,992 --> 00:04:04,173
Horizontally, X is a cosine with amplitude half the stroke; vertically,

53
00:04:04,193 --> 00:04:08,738
Y is a cosine too, shifted down by A minus h,

54
00:04:08,758 --> 00:04:14,012
so at its highest the dog is just h above the plate.

55
00:04:14,412 --> 00:04:22,312
Only while Y is above zero, with the dog showing above the plate, can it grip.

56
00:04:22,712 --> 00:04:30,199
With a lift amplitude of 1.1 millimetres and a dog height of 0.8,

57
00:04:30,219 --> 00:04:38,169
the half-width alpha is 74.2 degrees and the gripping interval 148.3:

58
00:04:38,189 --> 00:04:42,212
up at 325.8 degrees, down at 114.2.

59
00:04:43,732 --> 00:04:48,942
The cloth moves only while the dog is above the plate,

60
00:04:48,962 --> 00:04:56,398
so the feed per stitch is the dog's horizontal position at the drop minus its

61
00:04:56,418 --> 00:04:58,432
position at the rise.

62
00:04:58,832 --> 00:05:03,012
With the foremost point at the drop,

63
00:05:03,032 --> 00:05:11,412
s is S over two times one minus cos two alpha: here 0.926 of the stroke.

64
00:05:11,812 --> 00:05:17,592
So a 3 millimetre stitch needs a dog stroke of 3.24.

65
00:05:17,992 --> 00:05:24,106
The shortfall is the bit the dog travels forward below the plate before it emerges,

66
00:05:24,126 --> 00:05:25,732
which carries nothing.

67
00:05:26,132 --> 00:05:30,784
The stitch dial shows how far the cloth actually moves.

68
00:05:32,304 --> 00:05:36,799
This is the path of the tooth tip, a tilted ellipse;

69
00:05:36,819 --> 00:05:42,964
the thick part is where the dog is above the plate, carrying the cloth.

70
00:05:43,364 --> 00:05:45,784
What if the phase is wrong?

71
00:05:46,184 --> 00:05:51,081
On the left the horizontal motion alone is 30 degrees early:

72
00:05:51,101 --> 00:05:57,473
the dog reaches its front point early but doesn't drop for another 30 degrees,

73
00:05:57,493 --> 00:06:00,178
so above the plate it moves back,

74
00:06:00,198 --> 00:06:06,324
dragging the cloth back 0.22 millimetres and leaving a stitch of only 2.17.

75
00:06:06,724 --> 00:06:08,184
That's back-drag.

76
00:06:08,584 --> 00:06:14,287
Delay it instead and the dog is still moving forward as it reaches the plate,

77
00:06:14,307 --> 00:06:17,335
pushing the cloth after the needle is in.

78
00:06:17,735 --> 00:06:21,975
Both are fixed by the eccentric's mounting angle.

79
00:06:23,495 --> 00:06:24,515
Feed timing.

80
00:06:26,035 --> 00:06:30,455
Put the needle and the dog on one angle axis.

81
00:06:30,855 --> 00:06:40,282
Grey is the needle in the cloth, 101.7 to 258.3 degrees;

82
00:06:40,302 --> 00:06:50,235
green is the dog gripping, from 325.8 across zero to 114.2.

83
00:06:50,635 --> 00:06:56,522
They overlap only in the dozen or so degrees from 101.7 to 114.2,

84
00:06:56,542 --> 00:07:00,975
and by then the dog is almost at its front point,

85
00:07:00,995 --> 00:07:07,973
so the cloth moves only about 0.04 millimetres more, within the 0.05 allowed.

86
00:07:08,373 --> 00:07:12,993
There's plenty of margin on the rising side; the tight side is the drop.

87
00:07:14,513 --> 00:07:17,853
On the shop floor nobody measures angles.

88
00:07:18,253 --> 00:07:24,189
The manual's standard feed timing says: as the feed dog drops below the plate,

89
00:07:24,209 --> 00:07:29,993
its face and the top of the needle eye become flush with the plate together.

90
00:07:30,393 --> 00:07:35,932
In our example both happen at 114.2 degrees.

91
00:07:36,332 --> 00:07:40,061
Checking is easy: turn the handwheel, watch the plate,

92
00:07:40,081 --> 00:07:45,060
and see whether the dog face and the eye top sink out of sight together.

93
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:46,982
In this chapter,

94
00:07:47,002 --> 00:07:54,211
advancing or delaying the timing moves both the horizontal and lift motions

95
00:07:54,231 --> 00:07:55,078
together.

96
00:07:56,598 --> 00:08:00,338
Back to the thick cloth in our problem.

97
00:08:00,738 --> 00:08:05,141
Thicker cloth means the point meets it sooner.

98
00:08:05,541 --> 00:08:12,322
In the lab, switch to four plies of denim and the grey band widens,

99
00:08:12,342 --> 00:08:20,241
starting earlier: the point enters at 92.7 degrees, 9 earlier than thin cloth.

100
00:08:20,641 --> 00:08:28,256
But the feed timing hasn't changed, so the dog still drops at 114.2.

101
00:08:28,656 --> 00:08:32,212
For those twenty-odd degrees the needle is already in,

102
00:08:32,232 --> 00:08:34,596
and the cloth is still being pushed.

103
00:08:34,996 --> 00:08:36,856
How far?

104
00:08:37,256 --> 00:08:38,948
We'll calculate it at the end.

105
00:08:40,468 --> 00:08:43,880
Stitch length, dog height and the foot.

106
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:47,700
How is the stitch length set?

107
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:50,841
The lower end of the feed rod, C,

108
00:08:50,861 --> 00:08:54,772
is moved up and down about 2e by the eccentric,

109
00:08:54,792 --> 00:08:57,784
but it can only slide along a guide.

110
00:08:58,184 --> 00:09:03,580
Tilt the guide by theta and C also moves sideways 2e tan theta;

111
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:09,340
that's the dog's stroke, and times the feed efficiency, the stitch.

112
00:09:09,740 --> 00:09:16,490
Theta zero, no feed; tip it the other way and you get reverse sewing,

113
00:09:16,510 --> 00:09:20,120
which is what the reverse lever does.

114
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:23,785
With e of 3 millimetres and ratio 1,

115
00:09:23,805 --> 00:09:29,625
a 3 millimetre stitch needs 28.4 degrees and 5 millimetres 42.0,

116
00:09:29,645 --> 00:09:33,914
so the dial gets more crowded at long stitches.

117
00:09:35,434 --> 00:09:37,705
Ply shift and feed systems.

118
00:09:39,225 --> 00:09:43,005
The second big question: do both plies move together?

119
00:09:43,405 --> 00:09:48,612
Drop feed has one dog underneath, pushing the lower ply directly;

120
00:09:48,632 --> 00:09:53,438
the upper ply is carried only by friction between the plies,

121
00:09:53,458 --> 00:09:55,770
while the foot holds it back.

122
00:09:56,170 --> 00:09:57,171
In the lab,

123
00:09:57,191 --> 00:10:03,859
switch to two plies of slippery lining and watch the marks on the cloth:

124
00:10:03,879 --> 00:10:09,710
the upper marks fall further and further behind the lower ones.

125
00:10:10,110 --> 00:10:15,743
Just one percent short per stitch is 4 millimetres over 40 centimetres.

126
00:10:16,143 --> 00:10:18,950
Too little pressure and the cloth slips;

127
00:10:18,970 --> 00:10:21,707
too much and the upper ply shifts more.

128
00:10:22,107 --> 00:10:24,327
Drop feed is caught both ways.

129
00:10:25,847 --> 00:10:29,206
There are several feed systems that deal with ply shift.

130
00:10:29,606 --> 00:10:35,240
Differential feed uses two dogs to gather or stretch the same ply;

131
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:41,065
that's Lecture 9, and it doesn't help much with shift between plies.

132
00:10:41,465 --> 00:10:48,058
Needle feed has the needle swing with the dog once it's in the cloth,

133
00:10:48,078 --> 00:10:50,645
pinning the plies together.

134
00:10:51,045 --> 00:10:56,128
Compound feed adds a pair of alternating presser feet, carrying top,

135
00:10:56,148 --> 00:11:00,405
middle and bottom together; shift falls to about a tenth,

136
00:11:00,425 --> 00:11:03,857
and it's used for leather, bags and car seats.

137
00:11:04,257 --> 00:11:08,702
Top feed has its own dog, roller or belt above,

138
00:11:08,722 --> 00:11:13,357
so the top and bottom feed can be set separately.

139
00:11:14,877 --> 00:11:20,497
The dog's four motions are the gait of one leg of a walking robot.

140
00:11:20,897 --> 00:11:27,697
In stance the foot is on the ground and pushes back, moving the body forward;

141
00:11:27,717 --> 00:11:32,037
in swing it lifts and comes back through the air.

142
00:11:32,437 --> 00:11:37,162
The foot path combines the hip's swing and the knee's lift,

143
00:11:37,182 --> 00:11:42,068
just as the feed and lift eccentrics combine into an ellipse.

144
00:11:42,468 --> 00:11:46,968
The body advances only by the foot's travel while on the ground.

145
00:11:47,368 --> 00:11:50,497
Get the phase between the two joints wrong,

146
00:11:50,517 --> 00:11:56,136
and the foot is already swinging forward again after touching down: it slips.

147
00:11:56,536 --> 00:11:57,996
That's back-drag.

148
00:11:59,516 --> 00:12:01,889
An everyday example: rowing.

149
00:12:02,289 --> 00:12:06,640
The blade drives the boat only from the catch to the release;

150
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:11,513
after that it comes back through the air without touching the water.

151
00:12:11,913 --> 00:12:17,740
Release too late and the blade drags back in the water and slows the boat.

152
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:20,624
That's exactly the feed dog,

153
00:12:20,644 --> 00:12:27,242
pushing only while above the plate and back-dragging if it drops too late;

154
00:12:27,262 --> 00:12:30,640
the water surface is the throat plate.

155
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:33,542
Back to the start.

156
00:12:33,942 --> 00:12:38,922
With 4 millimetres of denim the point enters at 92.7 degrees,

157
00:12:38,942 --> 00:12:43,841
9 earlier than thin cloth; the dog doesn't drop until 114.2,

158
00:12:43,861 --> 00:12:50,071
and meanwhile the cloth is pushed about 0.11 millimetres with the needle in,

159
00:12:50,091 --> 00:12:54,498
over the 0.05 allowed, so the needle bends and breaks.

160
00:12:54,898 --> 00:13:01,523
Advance the feed timing, both motions together, by about 8 degrees:

161
00:13:01,543 --> 00:13:07,078
the drop moves to 106.2 and the movement falls to 0.045.

162
00:13:07,478 --> 00:13:12,866
Advance only the horizontal and 0.06 remains, with some back-drag.

163
00:13:13,266 --> 00:13:19,103
A 3 millimetre stitch needs a dog stroke of 3.24.

164
00:13:20,623 --> 00:13:26,198
